        "sections": [
          {
            "heading": "Risk levels tied to capability",
            "content": "Anthropic’s Responsible Scaling Policy defines AI Safety Levels to assess risk to society at different levels of model intelligence. Mann says Anthropic considered models to be at ASL-3, with some risk of harm but not significant. ASL-4 could involve significant loss of human life if a bad actor misuses the technology; ASL-5 could mean extinction-level risk through misuse or misalignment.",
            "citation_ids": [
              "S1",
              "S2"
            ]
          },
          {
            "heading": "Testing and communicating risks",
            "content": "Mann describes evaluating biological uplift by comparing what ASL-3 models could enable with Google Search, the previous state of the art in uplift trials. Anthropic found the models could meaningfully help someone trying to create a bioweapon, and it hired experts to evaluate such risks. His stated approach is to study the best models in laboratory settings to understand actual risks rather than assume they will be fine and wait for harm in the wild; he also says publishing findings can alert other labs and help legislators understand the risks.",
            "citation_ids": [
